1. Title
Implement a Railway Reservation System in C++ to Manage the Railway Passenger Waiting List Using a Circular Queue Implemented with Linked List.
2. Aim
To implement a Railway Passenger Waiting List using a Circular Queue with Linked List in C++, and perform insertion, deletion, display, and queue-status operations following the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle.
3. Objectives
To understand the concept of a Circular Queue. 
To implement a queue using a Singly Linked List. 
To add passengers to the waiting list when seats are full. 
To confirm tickets by removing passengers according to FIFO order. 
To handle overflow and underflow conditions. 
To display Passenger ID and Passenger Name. 

4. Theory
Queue-
A queue is a linear data structure that operates on the First In, First Out (FIFO) principle. This means that the first element added to the queue will be the very first one to be removed.

Types of Queues
Depending on the use case, queues can be adapted into different formats: 
Simple Queue: The standard linear queue where insertion happens strictly at the rear and deletion happens strictly at the front. 

Circular Queue: A queue where the last position connects back to the first position in a circle. This prevents memory wastage in array implementations by reusing empty spaces left behind by deleted items.

Priority Queue: A special queue where elements are assigned a priority value. Elements with higher priority are dequeued before lower-priority elements, regardless of when they arrived. 

Deque (Double-Ended Queue): A flexible queue where elements can be both inserted and deleted from either the front or the rear end.

Circular Queue
A Circular Queue is a queue in which the last position is connected back to the first position. It follows the FIFO principle.
In a railway reservation system, if all seats are occupied, new passengers can be added to a waiting list. When a confirmed passenger's seat becomes available, the passenger who has been waiting the longest is removed first.

5. Basic Operations
A. Insertion / Enqueue
A new passenger is added at the rear of the queue.
Example:
Front                         Rear
  ↓                             ↓
[101, Amit] → [102, Rahul] → [103, Priya]
       ↑___________________________|
Operations Performed
The following operations are performed in the railway waiting-list system:
6.1 Initialization
Initially, both front and rear pointers are set to NULL.
6.2 Enqueue / Insertion Operation
Purpose:
To add a new passenger to the rear of the waiting list.
Steps:
1.Create a new node. 
2.Store Passenger ID and Passenger Name. 
3.If the queue is empty, make the new node both front and rear. 
4.Otherwise, connect the new node after rear. 
5.Move rear to the new node. 
6.Connect rear->next to front to maintain the circular structure.


6.3 Dequeue / Deletion Operation
Purpose:
To remove the passenger from the front when the ticket is confirmed.
Since the queue follows FIFO, the passenger who entered first is removed first.
Steps:
1.Check whether the queue is empty. 
2.Store the front node in a temporary pointer. 
3.Move front to the next node. 
4.Connect rear->next to the new front. 
5.Delete the previous front node.
